"use strict";
// 비서 지식 카드(2026-10-18 1단계). AI 만 읽는 참고 자료라 영어로 쓴다(토큰이 적고 모델이 정확히 읽는다). 답은 플레이어 언어로 나온다.
// 기준: Stellaris 4.x(4.0 Phoenix 이후, 4.5 Cygnus). 패치마다 바뀌는 정확한 수치는 쓰지 않고 "게임 툴팁 확인"으로 돌린다.
// 질문의 낱말이 kw(한/영)와 맞는 카드 2~3장만 프롬프트에 넣는다(src/secretary.ts pickCards). 새 카드는 여기 추가만 하면 된다.
Object.defineProperty(exports, "__esModule", { value: true });
exports.SEC_CARDS = exports.CARD_VERSION = void 0;
exports.CARD_VERSION = 'Stellaris 4.x (4.5 Cygnus)';
exports.SEC_CARDS = [
    { id: 'energy', kw: ['에너지', '전력', '발전', '크레딧', 'energy', 'credits', 'power'], text: 'Energy deficit: when the stockpile hits zero, deficits cause penalties (stability/output debuffs, unpaid upkeep). Fixes, fastest first: sell surplus minerals/food/consumer goods on the Market (needs Market access); turn off or downgrade unneeded buildings and edicts; reduce fleet/army upkeep (dock fleets at a starbase with an anchorage, disband obsolete ships); then build generator districts and energy jobs (technicians). Long term: energy-focused worlds, trade income via Trade Policy, and techs that boost energy jobs. Check the top-bar energy tooltip to see the biggest upkeep sources.' },
    { id: 'minerals', kw: ['광물', '미네랄', '채굴', 'minerals', 'mining'], text: 'Minerals feed construction, alloys and consumer goods production. Deficit fixes: pause queued construction, sell other surplus on the Market, build mining districts/miners, and build mining stations on mineral deposits in owned systems (cheap, no pops needed). Mineral shortages usually come from too many foundry/factory jobs relative to miners; rebalance specialist vs worker jobs.' },
    { id: 'food', kw: ['식량', '음식', '농업', '굶', 'food', 'farm', 'starv'], text: 'Food deficit stops pop growth and can cause starvation penalties. Fixes: agriculture districts/farmer jobs, sell minerals for food on the Market, food-related edicts. Machine and lithoid empires may not need much food—check species traits. Gestalt machine empires do not use food for pops. Biological ships (BioGenesis) also draw on food in 4.5.' },
    { id: 'alloys', kw: ['합금', '함선 생산', 'alloy', 'alloys', 'foundry'], text: 'Alloys build ships, starbases and megastructures. They come from foundry/metallurgist jobs (mainly on industrial worlds), which consume minerals. If alloy income is near zero, you cannot keep up militarily: designate an industrial world, build foundries, and keep mineral income positive to feed them. War economies typically push alloys hard; peaceful builders still need a steady trickle for starbases and megastructures.' },
    { id: 'consumer', kw: ['소비재', '생필품', '상품', 'consumer goods', 'goods', 'artisan'], text: 'Consumer goods are needed by specialist pops, research jobs and many buildings. A deficit hurts research and stability. Fixes: civilian industry/artisan jobs, buy on the Market, reduce specialist-heavy buildings temporarily. Gestalt and some civics change consumer goods needs. Keep a small positive balance rather than a large stockpile.' },
    { id: 'stability', kw: ['안정도', '안정', '쾌적', '범죄', '불안', 'stability', 'amenities', 'crime', 'unrest'], text: 'Stability drives planet output; low stability causes penalties and eventually unrest. Main levers: amenities (entertainers/holo-theatres, amenity buildings), keeping factions happy (approval), living standards and policies, governors with stability traits, edicts, and avoiding unemployment and housing shortages. Crime is fought with enforcers/precinct houses. Check the planet stability tooltip for the biggest negative modifier.' },
    { id: 'pops', kw: ['인구', '팝', '성장', '이주', '주거', 'pop', 'pops', 'growth', 'migration', 'housing'], text: 'Since 4.0 population is handled as pop groups: a planet holds groups of pops by species/strata, and effects scale by pop size rather than per single pop. Growth needs food (for biological pops), housing and free capacity. More jobs than workers wastes buildings; more workers than jobs causes unemployment and instability. Spread growth via colonization, migration treaties and resettlement. Check the planet population tab for workforce vs jobs.' },
    { id: 'jobs', kw: ['일자리', '실업', '직업', '노동', 'jobs', 'unemploy', 'workforce', 'specialist'], text: 'Jobs need workforce. Unemployed pops lower stability; unfilled jobs waste upkeep. Fix unemployment by building districts/buildings that add the right jobs, or by resettling. Fix empty jobs by disabling those buildings or letting pops grow into them. Prioritize jobs that cover your current deficit (energy, minerals, food) before luxury outputs.' },
    { id: 'research', kw: ['연구', '기술', '과학', 'research', 'tech', 'science', 'physics', 'society', 'engineering'], text: 'Research comes from researcher jobs (labs, research districts on some worlds), the capital, starbase/research stations, and special planets. Boost with research-focused worlds, councilors/scientists with research traits, research agreements (federations in 4.5 can grant automatic research agreements), and relevant traditions/ascension perks. Research jobs need consumer goods—fix goods first. Tech cost scales with empire size, so sprawl slows research.' },
    { id: 'unity', kw: ['단결', '전통', '통합력', 'unity', 'tradition', 'traditions'], text: 'Unity buys traditions and powers some edicts and ascension perks. Sources: bureaucrat/priest/culture jobs, monuments, certain civics. Finishing tradition trees grants ascension perk slots. Early on, pick traditions that fix your weakest area (Expansion for growth, Prosperity for economy, Supremacy for war, Diplomacy for alliances).' },
    { id: 'influence', kw: ['영향력', '영향', '영유권', '전초기지', 'influence', 'claims', 'outpost'], text: 'Influence builds outposts, makes claims, pays for edicts/diplomacy and factions. It is scarce early. Raise it via faction approval (happy factions give influence), certain civics/councilors, and diplomatic traditions. Spend it carefully: outposts on chokepoints and valuable systems first. Branch offices cost influence (in 4.5 a flat base plus a distance penalty). Federation Code tech reduces diplomacy upkeep.' },
    { id: 'trade', kw: ['무역', '교역', '시장', '거래', 'trade', 'market', 'commercial pact'], text: 'Trade in 4.x is a resource converted by your Trade Policy (e.g., into energy or consumer goods/unity depending on policy). Commercial pacts and branch offices add trade. Use the Market to buy/sell resources at shifting prices—sell what piles up, buy only short-term emergencies. Trade League federations grant free commercial and wayline pacts among members (4.5).' },
    { id: 'war_exhaustion', kw: ['피로도', '전쟁 피로', '현상 유지', '항복', 'exhaustion', 'status quo', 'surrender'], text: 'War exhaustion rises from battles lost, ships destroyed and occupied planets. At 100% the side with maxed exhaustion can be forced into a status quo peace. To manage: avoid feeding fleets into losing fights, defend your own planets, occupy enemy planets, and consider offering status quo before collapse if you cannot win. High exhaustion also hurts stability. Wars give claims-based gains only for systems you claimed and hold.' },
    { id: 'war_prep', kw: ['전쟁', '선전포고', '침공', '함대', '군사력', 'war', 'declare', 'invade', 'fleet', 'military'], text: 'Before declaring war: compare fleet power (hover enemy fleets/empire diplomacy screen), make claims on the systems you want (influence), have armies to invade planets, and secure alloy income to rebuild. Choose a war goal that matches your aim. Defensive pacts and federations can drag in others—check their allies. Upgrade ship designs; in 4.5 Fleet Doctrine and weapon/defense preference policies steer auto-designs.' },
    { id: 'fleet_cap', kw: ['함대 수용', '해군 수용', '유지비', '정박', 'naval capacity', 'fleet cap', 'upkeep', 'anchorage'], text: 'Going over naval capacity sharply increases fleet upkeep. Raise capacity with anchorages on starbases, certain techs, traditions and buildings. Docking fleets at starbases with anchorages/related modules reduces upkeep in peacetime. Disband outdated ships rather than paying for obsolete fleets.' },
    { id: 'starbase', kw: ['스타베이스', '요새', '방어', '관문', 'starbase', 'defense', 'chokepoint', 'bastion'], text: 'Starbases control systems and defend chokepoints. In 4.5 starbase weapon modules are type-specific and upgrade automatically with tech. Fortify chokepoints bordering hostile empires; use shipyards/anchorages elsewhere. A single strong chokepoint starbase plus a fleet nearby is better than many weak ones.' },
    { id: 'diplomacy', kw: ['외교', '관계', '호감', '신뢰', '동맹', '조약', 'diplomacy', 'opinion', 'trust', 'alliance', 'pact', 'treaty'], text: 'Opinion is driven by ethics/policy compatibility, borders/rivalries, treaties, and envoys. Trust builds slowly over time with treaties. Envoys can improve relations. Use non-aggression pacts with dangerous neighbors, research agreements and commercial pacts with friends. Federations require good relations and compatible attitudes. Rivalries give influence but anger the rival and their friends.' },
    { id: 'federation', kw: ['연방', '연합', 'federation', 'federal'], text: 'Federations need a founding partner with positive relations. Federation types (e.g., galactic union, trade league, research cooperative, martial alliance, hegemony) give different perks that grow with cohesion and level. In 4.5 federation laws can grant members automatic commercial pacts, wayline pacts or research agreements; Trade Leagues give free commercial and wayline pacts. The president rotates per law; keep cohesion up via shared envoys and agreements.' },
    { id: 'galcom', kw: ['은하 공동체', '은하 의회', '결의안', '의제', 'galactic community', 'resolution', 'senate', 'council'], text: 'The Galactic Community votes on resolutions affecting all members. Diplomatic weight decides influence over votes. Propose resolutions that match your strengths (e.g., economic or research ones) and avoid ones that ban your playstyle. The Galactic Council gives veto/emergency powers to top members. Joining has costs (resolution compliance) and benefits (trade, protection).' },
    { id: 'vassal', kw: ['종속국', '속국', '충성도', '종주국', 'vassal', 'subject', 'loyalty', 'overlord', 'tributary'], text: 'Subjects (vassals, tributaries, protectorates, etc.) have loyalty. Low loyalty raises the chance they seek independence or join enemies. Raise loyalty by keeping agreement terms fair, sending envoys, granting benefits, and avoiding excessive demands. Holdings and specializations (Overlord) give extra value. If you are a subject, low loyalty toward your overlord risks punishment—balance independence plans with your strength.' },
    { id: 'factions', kw: ['파벌', '승인도', '지지율', '정치', 'faction', 'approval', 'support', 'ethics'], text: 'Factions form around ethics. Their approval rises when your policies, wars and actions match their demands; happy factions give influence and stability, unhappy ones reduce them. In 4.5 pop groups hold percentages of ethics/factions, so shifts happen more smoothly and more often. Address the largest unhappy faction first: check its demands in the faction screen and fulfil the cheapest ones (policy changes are often free).' },
    { id: 'espionage', kw: ['첩보', '스파이', '정보', '암호', 'espionage', 'spy', 'intel', 'infiltration', 'encryption'], text: 'Espionage needs an envoy assigned to build a spy network in a target empire. Network power unlocks operations (gather info, sabotage, steal tech, etc.). Intel level reveals more about the target. Defend with encryption and counter-intelligence; enemy networks against you can be weakened by codebreaking/encryption techs and assigning envoys to improve relations or counter.' },
    { id: 'ascension', kw: ['승천', '특전', '진화', '합성', '사이오닉', 'ascension', 'perk', 'synthetic', 'psionic', 'genetic', 'cybernetic'], text: 'Ascension perks come from finished tradition trees. Ascension paths (genetic/biological, cybernetic, synthetic, psionic) are major mid-game power spikes and have ethic/civic requirements. In 4.5 Biomorphosis completes about twice as fast. Pick perks that match your plan: economy perks early (e.g., planet-improving ones), path perks mid-game, megastructure perks once alloys flow.' },
    { id: 'crisis', kw: ['위기', '엔드게임', '프리토린', '언비든', '컨틴전시', 'crisis', 'endgame', 'prethoryn', 'unbidden', 'contingency'], text: 'Endgame crises (Prethoryn Scourge, Unbidden, Contingency, and others) arrive late and are far stronger than normal empires. Prepare: top-tier weapons/armor matching the crisis, maxed starbase chokepoints, large alloy reserves, allies. Fight at your chokepoints, not deep in their territory. Some crises can be weakened by destroying key structures (portals, hubs, the Contingency\'s sterilization hubs). Cooperate with other empires—the galaxy may unite.' },
    { id: 'colonize', kw: ['식민', '개척', '행성 개발', '거주', 'colon', 'colony', 'habitability', 'planet'], text: 'Colonize planets with good habitability for your species, or terraform/use other species. Each colony increases empire size (raising tech/tradition costs) but adds production long-term. Specialize planets (designations) so their districts and buildings reinforce one focus. In 4.x colony automation is smoother; still review new colonies so they do not build jobs you cannot fill.' },
    { id: 'leaders', kw: ['지도자', '평의회', '총독', '제독', '과학자', '특성', 'leader', 'council', 'governor', 'admiral', 'trait'], text: 'Leaders gain levels and traits; councilors give empire-wide bonuses depending on their position and traits. Put high-level leaders with matching traits on the council and on your most important planets/fleets. Leader cap is limited—do not hire leaders you cannot use. Veteran and paragon leaders can be very strong; keep them alive and in key roles.' },
    { id: 'edicts', kw: ['칙령', '정책', '법령', 'edict', 'policy', 'policies'], text: 'Edicts cost unity/energy/influence and give temporary or ongoing bonuses; the number of active edicts is limited by edict capacity. Policies are long-term settings (economic, military, diplomatic) that factions care about—changing a policy may anger or please factions and has a cooldown. Match edicts to your current bottleneck (e.g., a production edict during a deficit).' },
];
//# sourceMappingURL=secretary-cards.js.map